<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jumièges Abbey</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jumièges Abbey is a ruined Benedictine abbey on a loop of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> in Normandy, founded in the 7th century by Saint Philibert. Destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> and restored by the dukes of Normandy in the 10th century, it became a major monastery whose abbey church was consecrated in 1067 in the presence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>. Closed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> and used as a quarry, its ruins are among the finest of Romanesque Normandy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jumieges-abbey.jpg" alt="Jumièges Abbey" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tango7174 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Normandie_Seine_Jumieges1_tango7174.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The dukes restored <b>Jumièges</b> and Fécamp, Benedictine monks were installed at Mont-Saint-Michel in 966, and William of Volpiano reformed Fécamp from 1001.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
